In article <1993Sep10.014111.6246@twg.com>, blume@twg.com (David Blume) writes:
> My roommate and I have been playing Priests and Wizards recently, and
> we've been having our best games when wishing for:
> 
> blessed corrodeproof +2 gauntlets of power
> 
> GoP make a great defensive and offensive item.  The 3.1 "weight carrying"
> implementation makes having high strength so important that GoP have risen
> from our 4th favorite wish to our first favorite!
> 
> GoP are like getting a half and half combo of boots of speed and an artifact.
> You get to deny critters their extra hit (due to your being burdened) and you
> get a nice damage bonus due to your strength!
> 
> And here we were wishing for silly things like reflection, life saving,
> regeneration, artifacts, conflict, pet Archons and speed...
> 
Wait, wait... Don't try to sell this to people as an universal recipe
- it really all depends on what character you are playing, what strategy
you assume, and where in the game you are. If you have a naturally strong
character, the GoP don't help very much. In the late stages of the game
they help even less. And they are relatively easy to obtain without
wishing. Wait 'till you get to the level where critters start to walk with
wands of death - you'll learn to value reflection.

In almost all my games speed is more valuable than power - there's nothing
better than not giving the enemy any chance to hit you! And reflection is
essential for me - otherwise sooner or later I happen to run into a floating
eye....

